// A program that writes, which is a program that pulls Ch. 5 §1.5 out of the
// library — and one builtin out of the compiler.
//
// `print`, `print_char`, `print_int`, `char::to_utf8` and `char::utf8_len`
// are the prelude's own Trust and neither implementation wrote them. What
// they need between them is most of a language: an array local and an index
// that can fault, a tuple returned through `%sret` and taken apart by a
// hidden binding, a receiver taken by value, a `while` inside an `if` arm
// that is itself a statement, and the digit loop's `%` — which is the
// *symmetric* remainder, so a negative digit borrows from the next place.
//
// And one thing that is **not** Trust. `char::try_from(x)` is the single
// conversion into `char`, and the one thing in Ch. 5 §1 that cannot be
// written in this language: it has to make a `char` out of a word, which is
// exactly what no `as` does (Ch. 5 §1.2). It is four comparisons and four
// branches — not negative, no greater than U+10FFFF, and outside the
// surrogates, which are reserved for UTF-16 and are not characters.
//
// Its answer is an `Option<char>` whose `None` **costs no space at all**: a
// `char` stops at U+10FFFF, so the value above it is one the payload could
// never have held, and that is the discriminant (Ch. 2 §6). Building one
// writes that value; reading one tests against it and takes the variant that
// carries the payload by elimination. `unwrap` is where both happen, and its
// `None` arm is a `trap()` — the one call that does not come back.

fn digit(n: t27) -> char {
    char::try_from(n + 48).unwrap()
}

fn main() -> t27 {
    print("n = ");
    print_int(-407);
    print_char('\n');
    print_char('é');
    print_char(digit(7));
    println("");
    0
}
